00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:07.880 align:start position:0%
in what areas does the European  Commission and its Directorate General  for health and food safety carry out  audits primarily in the area of food  safety but also on food quality topics  such as organic production and quality  schemes secondly systems of animal  health and welfare and animal feed and  thirdly plant health and plant  protection products like pesticides the  audits cover production in the EU and  imports a key priority concerning plant  and animal health is to see if effective  systems for crisis preparedness and  management exist as disease outbreaks  can have serious implications for health  and the economy the Directorate General  for health and food safety also conducts  audits in human health areas on  organizations certifying medical devices  for people such as hip implants or  pacemakers on active pharmaceutical  ingredients from non EU countries and  importantly on measures aimed at  reducing antimicrobial resistance the  auditors also do a lot of non audit work  generating overview and other reports to  share important data this provides  feedback to the EU legislature in  updating current legislation or  developing EU policy for the future  based on its audit and analysis work the  auditors also contribute to training  initiatives for staff in EU and non-eu  countries to ensure that EU standards  are met they also liaised with non EU  food safety organizations to help design  international food safety and control  standards thus overall the European  Commission's audit work has an impact in  several air  by helping to ensure high levels of  safety through effective controls and  enforcement systems and feedback to  legislators it supports the global trade  of safe products facilitates jobs growth  and investment a deeper and fairer EU  internal market and most importantly  consumer safety
 

